content:
{
  "schema_version": 5,
  "type": "bugfix",
  "problem": [
    {
      "text": "The stated goal is equal K/V projection bitrates; two existing allocation candidates increased V alone.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc"]
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": [
    {
      "text": "The change raises K's increment to match V in two Q/K/V/O patterns, replacing [0,0,1,0] and [0,0,2,0] with [0,1,1,0] and [0,2,2,0]. It changes the candidate search space rather than enforcing equality after selection.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc", "call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58"]
    }
  ],
  "impact": [
    {
      "text": "For those candidates, matching K to V costs additional bits proportional to K's weight count. This can change the selected allocation and the surplus returned to the caller; MLP-only allocation is unaffected.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58", "call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc"]
    }
  ],
  "source_notes": [
    {
      "text": "The root conversion entry describes allocation as a fixed size-based heuristic rather than a calibration-error search, and records an exact-budget selection boundary.",
      "basis": "ancestor",
      "evidence": ["call_d3e43f3e396344aaa1295c43ae55f0c1"]
    }
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "No model conversion, GPU execution, quality evaluation or performance measurement was performed. Checks executed an extracted allocator with synthetic projection objects and the two substitutions applied in memory."
  ],
  "links": ["543c4b27714c6a677a6e714c452d62532f634cff"],
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "conversion",
      "title": "Pair K/V increments in the allocation heuristic",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "allocate_transformer orders attention projections as Q/K/V/O. Its pattern entries are integer bitrate increments above floor(effective budget per weight), with a minimum base of one and a per-projection cap of eight. Attention and MLP candidates are combined, costed using projection weight counts, and sorted for budget selection.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58"]
        },
        {
          "text": "Both direct callers, TransformerBlock.allocate_q and ParallelDecoderBlock.allocate_q, pass their attention projections in that order and return the strategy and surplus unchanged. They bypass allocation only when both attention and MLP are absent.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_3d418e2f67224b9985cdc583145f4bd1", "call_4e8ae04fca8b491d80ab31f83eea28fe"]
        },
        {
          "text": "With four 128-weight attention projections, target 3 and incoming surplus 383, the extracted parent allocator returned Q/K/V/O = 3/3/5/3; applying the patch returned 3/4/4/3. Both returned surplus 128: this example redistributes the same bit cost from V to K.",
          "basis": "execution",
          "evidence": ["call_ae68911a0b9a4bf0ac437a5ec3a34756"]
        },
        {
          "text": "The patch leaves both the unequal [0,1,2,1] candidate and the inherited exact-cost bisect boundary intact. Thus the change does not establish universal K/V equality or correct the previously documented budget boundary.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58", "call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc"]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "remaining-unequal-kv",
      "origin": "authoring_observation",
      "status": "suspected",
      "classification": "defect",
      "description": "A retained candidate can still allocate different K and V bitrates, contrary to the commit's stated goal.",
      "trigger": "Attention-only allocation with Q/K/V/O weight counts 256/128/128/256, target 3 and incoming surplus 640.",
      "expected_behavior": "K and V receive equal bitrates if the stated goal is universal.",
      "actual_behavior": "The patched allocator selects 3/4/5/4 and returns surplus 1.",
      "locations": [
        {
          "commit": "afac0a4320d9c4df31a8747ad1061da521698117",
          "path": "exllamav3/conversion/allocation.py",
          "symbol": "allocate_transformer"
        }
      ],
      "source_evidence": ["call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58", "call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc", "call_4e8ae04fca8b491d80ab31f83eea28fe"],
      "verification": [
        {
          "method": "repository_execution",
          "description": "Executed the extracted parent function with the two task substitutions and synthetic objects providing keys and weight counts.",
          "evidence": ["call_a3aa1d3a7efd4f9e9581298e45dd4e92"],
          "limits": "Not a complete conversion. Both inspected block callers expose this allocator; occurrence of these budgets in real models was not measured."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inherited-exact-budget-boundary",
      "origin": "authoring_observation",
      "status": "suspected",
      "classification": "defect",
      "description": "The inherited exact-cost candidate exclusion remains unchanged.",
      "trigger": "Four 128-weight attention projections, target 3 and incoming surplus 255 yield budget 1792, exactly matching the patched 3/4/4/3 candidate.",
      "expected_behavior": "Select the largest candidate within budget, including an exact match.",
      "actual_behavior": "bisect_right searches with (budget,), which sorts before equal-cost two-element tuples; allocation instead returns 3/3/3/3 with surplus 256.",
      "locations": [
        {
          "commit": "afac0a4320d9c4df31a8747ad1061da521698117",
          "path": "exllamav3/conversion/allocation.py",
          "symbol": "allocate_transformer"
        }
      ],
      "source_evidence": ["call_f8ecb2a14e2343669b960c63e6713c58", "call_2305816f3c424e77800e5a040e28b0bc", "call_3d418e2f67224b9985cdc583145f4bd1"],
      "verification": [
        {
          "method": "repository_execution",
          "description": "Executed the extracted, patched allocator on the exact-budget example.",
          "evidence": ["call_ae68911a0b9a4bf0ac437a5ec3a34756"],
          "limits": "Synthetic projection objects only. The condition applies through either inspected block caller when its budget matches a candidate cost."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Pairs K/V increments in two allocation candidates, but a remaining unequal candidate prevents a universal equality guarantee."
}
